docx-cli — third-party notices

This file lists the third-party software docx-cli redistributes (statically
linked into the published binaries via `bun build --compile`, or bundled
into `dist/index.js`), along with the attribution each license requires.

docx-cli itself is licensed under the MIT License (see LICENSE). Every
library it depends on is permissively licensed (MIT / ISC / Zlib /
BSD-3-Clause, or a dual license under which docx-cli elects a permissive
option), which leaves that license unchanged.

The standalone binaries additionally embed the Bun runtime, which includes
LGPL-licensed components; see "Bun runtime" below. The npm package
(dist/index.js) does not embed Bun: it runs on the Bun the user installs.

These files carry the attributions:

  THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES  the verbatim LICENSE text of EVERY npm package in the
                        production dependency closure (generated by
                        `bun run licenses`; a test fails while it is stale)
  NOTICES (this file)   the notices those LICENSE files don't contain (the
                        PDFium binary, the libxml2 C library compiled into
                        libxml2-wasm, the Bun runtime), the BSD-3-Clause
                        notices repeated for visibility, and the license
                        elections docx-cli makes
  LGPL-2.0.txt          the GNU Library General Public License v2 and the
  LGPL-2.1.txt          GNU Lesser General Public License v2.1 that cover
                        parts of the Bun runtime (shipped with the release
                        binaries, and in the source repository)

Whenever a runtime dependency is added or changed in package.json, run
`bun run licenses` and re-audit whether the new entry needs a notice here.


================================================================================
Bun runtime — embedded in the standalone binaries only
================================================================================

The release binaries (docx-<platform>) and `bun run build:binary`'s
dist/docx are produced by `bun build --compile`, which copies the Bun runtime
executable that runs the build and appends docx-cli's bundled JavaScript to
it. Each binary therefore contains a complete Bun runtime: the version the
release workflow installed (oven-sh/setup-bun) at the time of the build.

Bun                 MIT License. Source and license: https://github.com/oven-sh/bun
                    (LICENSE.md). License page: https://bun.com/docs/project/licensing

Bun's LICENSE.md states that Bun statically links JavaScriptCore and WebKit,
"which is LGPL-2 licensed", and that "WebCore files from WebKit are also
licensed under LGPL2". It also lists tinycc, under the LGPL v2.1, among the
libraries Bun statically links, together with permissively licensed libraries
(boringssl, brotli, c-ares, highway, libbase64, libdeflate, libicu,
libjpeg-turbo, libuv on Windows, libwebp, lol-html, ls-hpack, ls-qpack,
lsquic, mimalloc, picohttp, simdutf, uSockets, zlib-ng, zstd, and others);
that file is the authoritative list, with each library's license.

  JavaScriptCore / WebKit   LGPL-2 (GNU Library General Public License v2;
                            text in LGPL-2.0.txt, copied verbatim from
                            Source/JavaScriptCore/COPYING.LIB in Bun's fork)
                            Source: https://github.com/oven-sh/WebKit
  tinycc                    LGPL v2.1 (text in LGPL-2.1.txt, copied verbatim
                            from COPYING in Bun's fork)
                            Source: https://github.com/oven-sh/tinycc

Replacing the LGPL components. Everything needed to rebuild a docx-cli
binary on a modified Bun runtime is public. Bun's LICENSE.md gives the steps
for building Bun against a modified WebKit:

  git clone https://github.com/oven-sh/WebKit vendor/WebKit
  bun sync-webkit-source     # checks out the WebKit version that Bun pins
  bun run build:local        # outputs a new `bun` binary with your changes

Then build docx-cli with that runtime from docx-cli's source
(https://github.com/kklimuk/docx-cli, MIT):

  /path/to/your/bun install --frozen-lockfile
  /path/to/your/bun build --compile --outfile=docx src/index.ts

With no --target, `bun build --compile` embeds the `bun` executable that runs
it, so the resulting binary carries your modified runtime.


